{"authored" [{"question" "q-321074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t+1}{3},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-4)(1-t)^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "750a9b80ed9adc20e7a1121540bbe3a5a0844bd446168130d258d49d4d71c21b" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-321074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{t+1}{3}\\,⇒\\,t=3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=3x-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((3x-1)^{2}-4){(1-(3x-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x+1-4)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x-3)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3{x}^{2}-2x-1)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "750a9b80ed9adc20e7a1121540bbe3a5a0844bd446168130d258d49d4d71c21b" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-321074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75efc63830d8979ff86c80e34ac25462cd9c6d21797238664c242c3f6d3bab4b" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-321074" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "750a9b80ed9adc20e7a1121540bbe3a5a0844bd446168130d258d49d4d71c21b" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-321074" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "((3x-1)^2-4)(2-3x)^2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "750a9b80ed9adc20e7a1121540bbe3a5a0844bd446168130d258d49d4d71c21b" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "750a9b80ed9adc20e7a1121540bbe3a5a0844bd446168130d258d49d4d71c21b" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y-4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "750a9b80ed9adc20e7a1121540bbe3a5a0844bd446168130d258d49d4d71c21b" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75efc63830d8979ff86c80e34ac25462cd9c6d21797238664c242c3f6d3bab4b" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "750a9b80ed9adc20e7a1121540bbe3a5a0844bd446168130d258d49d4d71c21b" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "750a9b80ed9adc20e7a1121540bbe3a5a0844bd446168130d258d49d4d71c21b" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "750a9b80ed9adc20e7a1121540bbe3a5a0844bd446168130d258d49d4d71c21b" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "75efc63830d8979ff86c80e34ac25462cd9c6d21797238664c242c3f6d3bab4b" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/state.json" "file_sha256" "750a9b80ed9adc20e7a1121540bbe3a5a0844bd446168130d258d49d4d71c21b" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/example-5087.json" "file_sha256" "d02d5462908fc31ade5802f05e9f152c19c0bd10eca79ad08fe8da5b947be78b" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1.$\n\nFinally, since $t \\geq0$ and $x=2t^2,$ we must have $x \\geq 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0 .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/example-5087.json" "file_sha256" "d02d5462908fc31ade5802f05e9f152c19c0bd10eca79ad08fe8da5b947be78b" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/example-5101.json" "file_sha256" "549f7e93a2493d8d64f787800604628daefd5042825f50b3c38b437595295283" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/example-5101.json" "file_sha256" "549f7e93a2493d8d64f787800604628daefd5042825f50b3c38b437595295283" "basis" 1074 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997561/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-15654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t},\\,\\,\\,y=t(9-t),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{t}\\,⇒\\,t={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t={x}^{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ge 0$ and $x=\\sqrt{t},$ we must have $x\\ge 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6ff0a51ac75a21025e9d0e9a22eb512febb63f2fd44629e2b5f9555cf2074bb"} {"question" "q-15654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-2x(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0"] ["math" "y=-x(3+{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0"] ["math" "y=2{x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0"] ["math" "y={x}^{2}(3+{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0"] ["math" "y={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=5-t,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-1,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5-t\\,⇒\\,t=5-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=5-x$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(5-x)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =25-10x+{x}^{2}-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-10x+24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={x}^{2}-10x+24.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $x=5-t,$ we must have $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}-10x+24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6ff0a51ac75a21025e9d0e9a22eb512febb63f2fd44629e2b5f9555cf2074bb"} {"question" "q-15653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-4{x}^{2}-2x+5,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "y=-{x}^{2}+10x-24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "y=2{x}^{2}-5x-12,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "y=5{x}^{2}+x+2,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "y={x}^{2}-10x+24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={x}^{2}-10x+24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-321075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1-t}{4},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-1)(1+t)^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-321075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1-t}{4}\\,⇒\\,t=1-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1-4x$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((1-4x)^{2}-1)((1+(1-4x))^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-8x+16{x}^{2}-1)((2-4x)^{2}) \\\\  & =(-8x+16{x}^{2})(4)(1-2x)^{2} \\\\  & =8x(2x-1)(2-4x)^{2} \\\\  & =8x(2x-1)\\cdot 4(1-2x)^{2} \\\\  & =32x(2x-1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=32x(2x-1)^{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-321075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6ff0a51ac75a21025e9d0e9a22eb512febb63f2fd44629e2b5f9555cf2074bb"} {"question" "q-321075" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-321075" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-32x(1-2x)^3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-321074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t+1}{3},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-4)(1-t)^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-321074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{t+1}{3}\\,⇒\\,t=3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=3x-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((3x-1)^{2}-4){(1-(3x-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x+1-4)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x-3)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3{x}^{2}-2x-1)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-321074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6ff0a51ac75a21025e9d0e9a22eb512febb63f2fd44629e2b5f9555cf2074bb"} {"question" "q-321074" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-321074" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(3x-3)(3x+1)(2-3x)^2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t+1},\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{t-2},\\,t\\ne -1,\\,2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{t+1}\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{x}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t=\\frac{1}{x}-1$ into our expression for $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{((\\frac{1}{x}-1)-2)} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{x}-3)} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1-3x}{x})} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{1-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{x}{1-3x}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne -1,\\,2$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t+1},$ we must have $x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{1-3x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6ff0a51ac75a21025e9d0e9a22eb512febb63f2fd44629e2b5f9555cf2074bb"} {"question" "q-15677" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15677" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{3x}{1+2x},\\,x\\ne -\\frac{1}{2},\\,0"] ["math" "y=\\frac{3x}{1-2x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{1+x},\\,\\,x\\ne -1,\\,0"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{1-3x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{1-x},\\,\\,x\\ne 0,\\,1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15677" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{x}{1-3x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y-4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6ff0a51ac75a21025e9d0e9a22eb512febb63f2fd44629e2b5f9555cf2074bb"} {"question" "q-15751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{(\\frac{x+4}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+1}{2})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{(\\frac{x+4}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-1}{2})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{(\\frac{x-1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+4}{5})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{(\\frac{x-1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-24576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-24576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}) \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} \\\\ y & =-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-24576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6ff0a51ac75a21025e9d0e9a22eb512febb63f2fd44629e2b5f9555cf2074bb"} {"question" "q-24576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-24576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-18+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}"] ["math" "y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}"] ["math" "y=1+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{6}"] ["math" "y=5+{x}^{2}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{5}-{x}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-24576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-102687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-102687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡t & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =\\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, using the definition of a tangent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡t}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-102687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6ff0a51ac75a21025e9d0e9a22eb512febb63f2fd44629e2b5f9555cf2074bb"} {"question" "q-102687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-102687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{2x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{2x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}"] ["math" "y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-102687" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6ff0a51ac75a21025e9d0e9a22eb512febb63f2fd44629e2b5f9555cf2074bb"} {"question" "q-15752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}=2\\pi "] ["math" "{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x+3 \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a6ff0a51ac75a21025e9d0e9a22eb512febb63f2fd44629e2b5f9555cf2074bb"} {"question" "q-15657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1"] ["math" "(x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=3"] ["math" "3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "q-15657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/state.json" "file_sha256" "747c14105b08e29c7053bb18b486ca3fbfbc37635ef202dd81c59fb3d9e87190"} {"question" "e-5087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/example-5087.json" "file_sha256" "d02d5462908fc31ade5802f05e9f152c19c0bd10eca79ad08fe8da5b947be78b"} {"question" "e-5087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1.$\n\nFinally, since $t \\geq0$ and $x=2t^2,$ we must have $x \\geq 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0 .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/example-5087.json" "file_sha256" "d02d5462908fc31ade5802f05e9f152c19c0bd10eca79ad08fe8da5b947be78b"} {"question" "e-5101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/example-5101.json" "file_sha256" "549f7e93a2493d8d64f787800604628daefd5042825f50b3c38b437595295283"} {"question" "e-5101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039508/example-5101.json" "file_sha256" "549f7e93a2493d8d64f787800604628daefd5042825f50b3c38b437595295283"}] "usage" {"e-5087" [] "e-5101" [] "q-15751" [] "q-15752" [] "q-321074" []} "basis" 1364 "retractions" [] "imm